WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.960
STEREN GIANNINI: Hi, I am
Steren Giannini, Senior Product

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.760
Manager at Google Cloud.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.030
Welcome to "What's
New in Serverless?"

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.490
Today, we are mainly
going to talk about two

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:25.390
of our Serverless products,
Google Cloud Functions, which

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.090
is Serverless functions,
and Google Cloud

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:34.000
Run, which is Serverless
applications and containers.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.780
First, I want to call
out that Serverless

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.360
is available in every
Google Cloud region.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.710
You will find Clarin and
Cloud Functions in all

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.140
of the Google Cloud regions.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.950
And you can expect us to open up
those products in future Google

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.480
Cloud regions.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.470
All right.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.702
Let us dive into
Cloud Functions.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:06.970
So, first, we've been
hard at work delivering

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.990
a new runtimes for Cloud
Functions, new programming

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:13.360
languages.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.070
The first one is Ruby.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:26.780
then is .NET Core, PHP, and
we've also been updating

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:32.750
our existing runtimes with
Python 3.9 and Node.js 14,

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.760
and even 16 in preview.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.640
Next, we added
more customization

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.660
to the build process.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.620
So you might know that when you
deploy your function on Cloud

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.950
Function, the first
step that happens

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.490
is that this function is
built before it gets deployed.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.570
So you can now
customize where that

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:04.060
build happens by targeting
a private worker pool.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.490
This allows you to
customize the machine

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.520
types on which the
function will be built,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.810
or to ensure that the function
is built within your VPC SC

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.560
perimeter.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.410
We've also added the
ability to define

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.640
build environment variables.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.460
So these en vars are en vars
available at build time.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.320
They, for example, help
you configure the buildback

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:29.820
behavior.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:40.230
And, finally, we delivered Min
instances to Cloud Functions.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:46.210
So we've been hearing from you
that cold start is a problem

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.840
with function as a service.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.020
So what is a cold start?

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.150
When your function
receives no traffic,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:57.940
its number or function
instances are scaled to 0.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.050
But then when traffic
arrives, Cloud Functions

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.410
will scale from 0
to 1, and the time

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.120
it takes for the
function to start

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.040
is what we call a cold start.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.690
Thanks to Min instances, you
can simply define a value

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.810
that Cloud Functions
will scale down to,

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.900
keeping one or more
instances warm,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.570
so that when the next
request comes in,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:28.800
there are already some warm
instances to process them.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.580
So when not in use,
those warm instances

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:38.680
are charged for memory, but
also for CPU at 10% the price.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.010
To use Min instances, you can
do so via a simple command line

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.590
flag or user interface inputs.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:50.210
Right now, I would
like to call out

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.460
two of the Google
Cloud orchestration

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.970
products that pair very well
with Cloud Functions and Cloud

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:57.510
Run.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:01.410
The first one is
called Eventarc.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.520
So Eventarc allows you to
asynchronously deliver events

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.880
from Google Services
software as a service,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.150
and you own apps to
Serverless products.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.480
Eventarc has added
a few new features--

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.890
the ability to target Cloud
Functions, the ability

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:25.240
to receive events
from Cloud Storage,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.820
and a new standalone
user interface that

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.800
allow you to manage
your Eventarc

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.585
triggers in a central place.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:38.830
Second, I want to
call out Workflows,

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.600
which is Google Cloud product
to orchestrate automate Google

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.120
Cloud and HTTP based API
services with a workflow

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.710
that you define.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.380
So on the right, you can
see a screenshot of the user

00:04:54.380 --> 00:05:00.110
interface where the workflow is
using a comprehensible syntax,

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:05.450
but can also be visualized
in a very nice graph.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:09.290
So Workflows has added
a few new features.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:15.810
First, the ability to use
HTTP callbacks, the ability

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.860
to connect to many
Google Cloud APIs.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.370
It has increased the memory,
as well as the number

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.500
of concurrent executions.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.610
And there were many syntax
and library enhancements.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:33.480
All right.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:38.410
Now let us dive into Cloud Run.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.050
So, first, I want
to call out that we

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:46.480
have added committed use
discounts to Cloud Run.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.400
So committed use
discounts allow you

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.890
to commit to an early spend
on Cloud Run for a year,

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:58.300
and you would-- you simply
receive a 17% discount

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.920
on this commitment.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:05.060
Of course, usage above
the committed amount

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.220
is charged at normal rates.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.200
This is self-service.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.660
From the Cloud Console,
you go into Billing,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.460
you click Commitment,
and this is

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.710
where you can purchase
the commitment for a year.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:22.780
I should note that committed
use discounts apply

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:26.590
to all Cloud Run
services of your accounts

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.860
across all of your projects
for a given region.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.620
Let's take an example.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:38.270
On the right, we can see
an example Cloud Run bill

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.850
over time.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.340
This, basically, reflects the
Cloud Run usage over time.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:51.410
As you can see, because most of
the time, the bill is above $1,

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:54.320
it makes it very economical
in that situation

00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:00.140
to commit to a $1 per
hour usage, because that

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:07.090
means that you would only
pay $0.83 for every hour.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.210
And of course, if usage
goes above the commitment,

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:16.150
for example, because you
used $1.50 of Cloud Run,

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.054
then you would only pay
the discounted on the $1.00

00:07:20.054 --> 00:07:27.430
and $0.50 above the dollar.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:31.930
Now, I want to tell you how we
have been improving the Cloud

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.860
Run developer experience
with three things following

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.080
the development journey.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.390
First, we have added local
development to Cloud Run.

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:47.770
Second, we are making it easier
to deploy your source code.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:51.610
And third, we improved
the observability

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.990
of your Cloud Run services.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:55.770
Let's take a look.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:01.820
Local development-- this allows
you to run your Cloud Run

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:05.940
services in a local emulator.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.780
This emulator is available
in the gcloud command line,

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.010
in the Cloud Code for VSCode,
in Cloud Code for IntelliJ

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.260
as well as in
Cloud Shell Editor,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.200
the online IDE of Google Cloud.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.660
If we take an
example, you can see

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:28.100
that you can store your Cloud
Run config in a file that

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.580
would allow you to define
how the emulator should

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.289
be configured.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:41.150
Then, using a simple command,
gcloud beta code dev,

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:44.450
you start a local
development environment that

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:48.680
will emulate the
characteristics of Cloud Run,

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.990
with the CPU and
memory allocation

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.720
that you defined with
the environment variables

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.280
that you defined.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.660
And it will watch your local
source code for changes,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.450
and when they happen,
will rebuild them

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:07.440
into a container that-- and
restart the local server.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.380
So quite handy for
a fast development

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:12.560
group instead of
deploying to the Cloud

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.140
for testing new changes.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:20.470
Then, once you have
developed your service,

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:22.180
you want to deploy it.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:25.900
We have added support
for deploying source code

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.870
to Cloud Run.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.170
This is, basically, a command,
a very simple command,

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:38.260
in three words that will build,
push, and deploy your service

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.000
from local source code.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.710
This command is
gcloud run deploy.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:45.460
That's right.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.830
That's the default
for gcloud run deploy.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:52.450
It's to build your local
source code using Cloud Build,

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:56.290
push it to Artifact
Registry, and then deploy it

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:57.160
to Cloud Run.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.440
This command is also leveraging
Google Cloud Buildpacks,

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:07.890
in order to allow you to
deploy source code in popular

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.970
languages without any
Dockerfile, that includes Go,

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:15.750
Node.js, Python, Java, and .Net.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.120
But, of course, if a local
Dockerfile is present,

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.630
then this command
will simply build it

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.000
with the instructions that
are in the Dockerfile,

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.505
and deploy the arbitrary
container image to Cloud Run.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.850
Lastly, we added more
observability to Cloud Run.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:39.180
First, by delivering
a few more metrics,

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:41.910
notably, the instance
count metric,

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:44.040
which would show you
simply the number

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:50.910
of active and idle instances
for a given Cloud Run service.

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:54.450
Then, we've walked with
the Cloud AR Reporting team

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:58.890
to make sure AR reporting
was able to capture

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:02.510
system errors out of the box.

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:04.860
So without any
more configuration,

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:09.110
that means that, if your Cloud
Run container instance is out

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.810
of memory, or if
Cloud Run is not

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:14.810
able to scale because it is
reaching its max instance

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:18.650
limit, those errors that
are present in your logs

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:22.670
will now also be aggregated
in Cloud error reporting

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:26.000
and displayed in a very
actionable way in the Cloud Run

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.320
user interface.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:33.670
And lastly, we have collaborated
with the Cloud Trace team

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:39.100
to provide out-of-the-box
request tracing.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:43.020
That means that you can analyze
the latency of your Cloud Run

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:47.515
services using Cloud Trace
without any instrumentation

00:11:47.515 --> 00:11:48.015
needed.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.100
All right, now, I'm
going to tell you

00:11:54.100 --> 00:12:00.560
how we have made it easier to
run more workloads on Cloud

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.490
Run with three changes.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:07.840
First, giving you more control
around the CPU allocation

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:09.460
of your service.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:13.390
Second, by delivering a
new execution environment.

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:17.170
And third, by pushing
the limits that

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:19.450
were historical
limits of Cloud Run.

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:21.470
Let's take a look.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:26.660
CPU allocation--
let's take an example.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:32.880
Here, you can see one container
instance of a Cloud Run

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:34.080
service.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.610
It is starting.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:36.810
Then it has started.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:40.500
It receives requests,
potentially multiple requests

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.960
at the same time
thanks to concurrency.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:47.380
And then at the end of its
life, it gets terminated.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:52.710
Today, the CPU for
that container instance

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:57.780
would only be allocated when the
container instance is starting,

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.465
or when it is receiving
at least one request.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.730
As a customer, you
would also only

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:09.850
be charged when the
CPU is allocated.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:13.660
And, in particular, you are
charged for CPU and memory,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:17.515
but also you are charged
every time a request arrives.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:25.360
We are introducing the
ability to opt out of this CPU

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:30.400
throttling by simply saying
that you want the CPU to always

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:33.190
be allocated to your
controller instances

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:38.080
as long as the
instance is alive.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:41.680
So in that case, you would
be charged, of course,

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:46.450
for the entire lifetime of
that continual instance.

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:50.470
But the CPU will always be
available to that container

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.430
instance.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:54.610
You can enable this
via simple command line

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:57.310
flag on an existing
or a new service

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:01.760
as well as a simple checkbox
in the user interface.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.230
So this new option to
always have CPU allocated

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:08.950
allows you to run background
tasks or async processing

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:13.510
after you have
returned requests.

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:16.720
It makes Cloud Run more
compatible with monitoring

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.880
agents like OpenTelemetry.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:24.080
Many programming languages
do things asynchronously,

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.070
like Goroutines, or async,
or threads, or coroutines.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:32.190
These are now more
compatible with Cloud Run.

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:34.470
And lastly, you can
deploy Spring Boot apps

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:38.810
that are expecting to
do background things.

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:43.160
And as I said, when you opt
in to this new CPU allocation

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:46.340
to always have
CPU allocated, you

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.530
are charged for a
different price.

00:14:48.530 --> 00:14:50.720
There is no request fee.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:56.910
And the CPU and
memory is 25% lower.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.120
You can couple
this always on CPU

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:03.840
allocation with Min
instances, basically

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:07.500
allowing you to have
a number of instances

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:13.730
that are always scheduled,
and which always have CPU,

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:15.860
and looking at some quite
interesting use cases.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:23.060
Next, you will find
that we've improved

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:26.780
the performances of
Cloud Run, as well

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:31.130
as we are adding network
file system support.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:35.090
This is thanks to a new
execution environment

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:37.830
that we call Second Generation.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.970
So, basically, when you deploy
a container image to Cloud Run,

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:44.750
it runs in the execution
environment, also known

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.080
as Sandbox.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:50.620
So, today, you can opt into
the Second Generation execution

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:51.670
environment.

00:15:51.670 --> 00:15:56.110
Again, using a simple command
line flag or user interface

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:57.250
button.

00:15:57.250 --> 00:16:00.220
And this new sandbox
has increased network

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:04.420
in CPU performance, has
better Linux compatibility,

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:08.260
and allows you to use network
file system, like Cloud

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:09.640
Filestore, for example.

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:15.800
And, next, I should call
out that we are constantly

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:21.150
at work to increase the
limits of Cloud Run.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.320
So, first, we have added
support for more protocols,

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:29.510
like Websockets, HTTP/2, and
gRPC bi-directional streaming.

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:33.000
All of these are
generally available now.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:38.900
You can now pick up to 16
gigabytes of memory in preview,

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:42.180
up to 4 VCPUs.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:47.890
Each request can run
for up to 16 minutes.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.340
And each container
instance can process up

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:55.060
to 1,000 concurrent
requests at the same time.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:57.250
This can become
very handy if you

00:16:57.250 --> 00:17:00.550
are using Websockets and
opening many streams on a given

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:01.525
instance, for example.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.819
Lastly, I want to tell
you how we have made Cloud

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:11.650
Run even easier to secure--

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:14.200
by meeting regulatory
frameworks,

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:17.890
by allowing you to put a secure
perimeter around your Cloud Run

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:22.600
services, and by helping you
secure your Cloud Run services.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:30.440
So Cloud Run is now covered by
these regulatory frameworks.

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:34.250
We know this is very important
for enterprise settings.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:39.140
I should call out FedRAMP
Moderate, PCI DSS, SOC 1, 2,

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:40.760
and 3.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:48.340
All of these are now
covered for Cloud Run.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:53.260
Next, how can you
secure or define

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:58.010
what is allowed to reach
your Cloud Run service?

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:02.120
Well, first, we have added
support for ingress control.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:06.290
By default, all traffic
at a networking layer

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:09.230
is allowed to reach
your Cloud Run service.

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:12.860
But you can now restrict
the ingress to only allow

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:16.880
internal traffic, so that means
traffic coming from your VPC

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:21.090
or from Google Cloud,
like Cloud Webserve,

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:25.320
and you can allow internal
traffic plus traffic coming

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:29.430
from Google Cloud
Load Balancing.

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:35.160
Similarly, you can control where
the unknown request should go.

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:38.100
For example, when you
do an outbound request

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:43.590
to the internet, you can force
it to go through your VPC.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:47.290
And by using ingress
and egress settings,

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:50.680
you can also enable
VPC service control,

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:55.410
allowing you to put your Cloud
Run services inside a VPC AC

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:58.190
perimeter.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.740
Thanks to Cloud Run Integration
with Google Cloud Load

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:03.470
Balancing, you can
leverage Cloud Armor, which

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.130
is a web application firewall.

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:09.040
And you can use
identity aware proxy

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:11.500
to build internal
applications that

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:15.160
would only be accessible to
your employees, for example.

00:19:18.232 --> 00:19:19.690
And, lastly, I want
to tell you how

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:24.080
we made it easier to secure
your Cloud Run services.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:29.120
First, by providing a native
integration with Google Cloud

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:31.690
Secret Manager.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:34.150
So Secret Manager is
a Google Cloud product

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:38.500
to store your secrets
in a secured place

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:41.350
with potentially your
own encryption keys.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:45.640
You can now mount those
secrets as volumes or as

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:49.390
en vars inside your
Cloud Run services,

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:51.700
allowing you to
decouple your code,

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:54.310
your container from
its configuration,

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:55.630
or even its secret.

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:02.590
Then, we added more
security features.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:06.220
Of course, you might know
that Cloud Run services run

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:08.560
with a certain
identity, so that means

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:11.350
you can provide a
service account that

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:14.230
would be used as the
identity of the code that

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:17.380
runs for a particular service.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:21.850
And the service account can have
more or less IM permissions.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:25.720
Notably, you can remove all
permission of a certain Cloud

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:29.410
Run service if this one is
not expected to call any APIs.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.730
We are announcing the
general availability

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:39.270
of Binary Authorization
for Cloud Run.

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:44.390
Binary Authorization allows you
to define a policy regarding

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:50.660
which containers can or cannot
be deployed to Cloud Run.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:54.080
And by using an
organizational policy,

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:57.020
you can enforce
Binary Authorization

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:02.090
to be applied to your Cloud Run
services in a set of projects.

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:06.250
Lastly, we delivered customer
manage encryption keys,

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:08.260
allowing you to bring
your own encryption

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:14.200
keys to encrypt the container
images deployed to Cloud Run.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.330
And finally, we
haven't stopped here.

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:19.570
We are proactively
producing recommendations

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:22.580
to secure your
Cloud Run services.

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:24.220
The first one that
we delivered is

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:26.920
to recommend you to
use a dedicated service

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:31.300
account with a minimal set of
permissions for each production

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.155
Cloud Run services.

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:40.040
So to recap, we have added
many things to Cloud Run.

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:42.290
The first is committed
used discount,

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.320
allowing you to get a
discount of up to 17%

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.270
on the amount you commit.

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:50.180
We've improved, and we
are continuously improving

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:53.330
its developer experience,
with local development,

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:56.960
deploying from source
code, more metrics.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:58.760
We've increased the
workloads that you

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:02.390
can deploy to Cloud
Run, with CPU allocation

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:05.720
controls, a Second Generation
execution environment,

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:09.410
or just by pushing the
limits of Cloud Run.

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:12.990
And, lastly, we made it easier
to secure your Cloud Run

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:13.490
services.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:19.200
To learn more
about Serverless, I

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:21.100
recommend you those
three sessions--

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:23.610
Dev100, which is a
great introduction

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:27.570
to Serverless on GCP;
Dev201, which will deep

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:30.660
dive into some very cool
features of Cloud Functions,

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:34.860
including some that
are coming; and Dev205,

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:38.940
which will teach you how to
build event-driven applications

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:42.470
with Eventarc and Cloud Run.

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:44.190
Thanks for joining me today.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:46.250
This was "What's
New in Serverless?"

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:49.180
Enjoy the rest of Cloud Next.

